  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nWater Vapor and upper air models show southwest flow aloft, as\nridging sits over the northern and central plains. Upper low\ncurrently sits over the west coast, while a shortwave currently\nsits over WY riding the southwest flow into the Dakotas. 18Z \nsurface analysis shows associated surface low over the CO/WY\nborder. Winds are currently out of the southeast, with temps \nranging from around 60 to mid 70s this afternoon. Radar currently\nhas patchy precipitation over the southern half of the CWA as the\ndisturbance moves into the region. \n\nShortwave will continue to lift into the Dakotas today, bringing\nwidespread precipitation with it. HREF 24 hour probabilities for \n0.5\" or more QPF show the highest chances (60-95%) over south- \ncentral SD. Much of the area could at least see wetting rain \nthrough Friday morning. Some areas could see some lightning at\ntimes with this precipitation, however severe storms are not\nanticipated.\n\nFriday will be mostly dry as the shortwave exits the area early\nmorning. Shortwave ridging sets up over the northern plains, but \nby Saturday the aformentioned upper low shifts over the Rockies \nbringing cooler temperatures and more potential for widespread \nprecipitation Saturday and Sunday. A bit more uncertainty with\nQPF, as much will depend on the track of the low as it moves into\nthe northern/central plains, but currently the wettest day looks\nto be Sunday. Decent chance that some areas will see more wetting\nrains, with potential for much of the area to see upwards of a\nhalf inch Sunday into Monday morning. \n\nBy the latter half of monday, upper ridge builds over the plains,\nbringing a warming trend Monday through much of next week.\nTemperatures climb back to at or above normal by Tuesday and\nWednesday.",
